"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 3.7 and an ISUP grade group 5 Gleason pattern from the prostatectomy specimen. Contributing to risk are the pathological T3a stage and documented extraprostatic extension. Countervailing findings include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no detected lymph node metastasis. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the initial biopsy are missing. Nodal status was assessed with no metastasis found. Prognostic uncertainty remains due to the lack of postoperative PSA data and follow-up information. Future trajectory assessment would be significantly revised by obtaining serial postoperative PSA measurements and understanding their subsequent patterns. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 47.09–78.49 months; its coverage has not been validated."
